.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAM-GRANT-ROLE 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eam-grant-role \- give a role to a user or a user group
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eam grant-role
.BR \-\-role | \-o
.I NAME
.BR \-\-principal | \-p
.I ERN
[\fB\-\-namespace\fR|\fB\-e\fR \fINAME\fR...]
[\fB\-\-resource\fR|\fB\-u\fR \fIERN\fR...]
[\fB\-\-account\fR|\fB\-a\fR \fIID\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Gives a role to a user or a user group, scoped to namespaces and
resources. The role says what may be done; this says by whom and where.
.PP
What a user may do is the union of every grant held by them and by every
group they belong to. There are no deny rules and no role hierarchy: a
grant only ever adds, and something nobody granted is refused. To take
something away, revoke the grant that gave it \-
.BR euclid-cli-eam-revoke-role (1).
.SH THE PRINCIPAL
One option for both users and groups, because the ERN already says which:
.RS
.nf
ern:eam:eu-central-1:000000000000:user:jdoe
ern:eam:eu-central-1:000000000000:userGroup:developers
.fi
.RE
.PP
Granting to a group is how a grant outlives the people it applies to: a
user added to the group later holds it without anybody revisiting this
command. Groups are flat \- a group is never a member of another \- so
the set of grants a user holds is their own plus those of the groups
they are directly in, and nothing deeper.
.SH SCOPE
.TP
.B \-\-namespace
The namespaces of the account it applies in. A single
.I *
means every namespace of that account, including ones created later.
Repeat the option for several. A grant that applies in no namespace
grants nothing, so an empty list is refused rather than stored as a
silent no-op.
.TP
.B \-\-resource
ERN patterns it applies to, for the actions that name a resource. A
pattern is exact, or ends in
.I *
and matches by prefix \-
.I "ern:ens:eu-central-1:000000000000:production:topic:order\-*"
covers
.IR order\-events .
A trailing
.I *
is the only wildcard there is; one in the middle matches nothing. A
single
.I *
means every resource.
.PP
Actions that name no resource \- listing, creating, metadata \- are not
narrowed by
.BR \-\-resource .
The patterns apply to the actions that do.
.PP
Both default to
.IR * .
A grant that says neither applies account-wide, which is the reading that
matches "I granted them this role" rather than the one where an
unmentioned scope silently matches nothing.
.SH SCOPE IS FIXED ONCE WRITTEN
There is no way to widen or narrow a grant. Revoke it and grant again \-
which is why
.BR euclid-cli-eam-revoke-role (1)
takes the grant's id, and why granting the same role to the same
principal twice with different scope is a normal thing to do rather than
a mistake.
.PP
It also means a grant naming several namespaces is all-or-nothing:
removing one of them means revoking the grant and re-granting the rest.
.SH BOTH HAVE TO EXIST
The role and the principal are looked up before the grant is written. A
grant naming either one that does not exist would be inert, and nobody
would find out until somebody was refused something they were told they
had.
.PP
A role name resolves to your account's own role first, then to a built-in
one \- the same order
.BR euclid-cli-eam-get-role (1)
resolves in, and the order the grant itself will resolve in every time it
is evaluated. The role is read at authorization time, not copied here, so
a later
.BR euclid-cli-eam-update-role (1)
changes what this grant allows.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-role " " \fINAME\fR
Role name. A built-in name also works. Required.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-principal " " \fIERN\fR
Who gets it: a user ERN or a user-group ERN. Required.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-namespace " " \fINAME\fR
Namespaces it applies in. Defaults to \fI*\fR.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-resource " " \fIERN\fR
ERN patterns it applies to. Defaults to \fI*\fR.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-account " " \fIID\fR
Account to grant in. Your own unless given.
.SH WHO MAY RUN IT
Installation administrators \- members of the
.I administrator
user group. See
.B ADMINISTRATOR PRIVILEGES
in
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The grant was created. Its JSON representation is printed, including the
.I grantId
that
.BR euclid-cli-eam-revoke-role (1)
takes \- the only handle there is on it, so keep it or look it up again
with
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-grants (1).
.TP
.B 1
No role or no principal given, no role of that name, no user or user
group with that ERN, an empty namespace list, or the caller is not an
administrator. If the failure was an expired or invalid session
(\fIHTTP 401\fR), the stored credentials file is removed; run
.B euclid-cli eam login
again before retrying.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# Let a group read everything in one namespace
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam grant-role \e
    --role reader \e
    --principal ern:eam:eu-central-1:000000000000:userGroup:support \e
    --namespace production

# One service, one namespace, the topics it owns and no others
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam grant-role \e
    --role order-service \e
    --principal ern:eam:eu-central-1:000000000000:user:svc-orders \e
    --namespace production \e
    --resource 'ern:ens:eu-central-1:000000000000:production:topic:order-*'

# Account-wide: neither scope named
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam grant-role \e
    --role operator \e
    --principal ern:eam:eu-central-1:000000000000:user:jdoe

# Two namespaces in one grant - revoking either means revoking both
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam grant-role \e
    --role publisher \e
    --principal ern:eam:eu-central-1:000000000000:user:jdoe \e
    --namespace staging --namespace production
.fi
.RE
.PP
Quote patterns containing
.I *
so the shell does not expand them against the working directory.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-revoke-role (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-grants (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-check-permission (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-roles (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-get-role (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-create-role (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-user-group-add-user (1)
